data.content:
Here's the output — property names are PascalCase as requested:

```json
{
  "Name": "Alice",
  "Age": 30
}
```

The key ingredient is `JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase`, which was added in .NET 9. Since your record's constructor parameters are lowercase (`name`, `age`), C# generates PascalCase backing properties (`Name`, `Age`) by default — so `PascalCase` already matches. But setting the policy explicitly makes the intent clear and protects you if you ever mix in `camelCase`-named properties.

The full `Program.cs`:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

var person = new Person("Alice", 30);

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase,
    WriteIndented = true
};

string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options);
Console.WriteLine(json);

public record Person(string name, int age);
```
